Mallgothic Chime
Ingredients: BLACK METAL + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Xylophone
Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a ghost mall where teenagers rewired abandoned arcade cabinets.

Mallgothic Chime sounds like a freezing draft sweeping through a derelict food court at 3 AM. The frantic, percussive clatter of a dismantled 1980s brass-bar xylophone cascades over a wall of tremolo-picked, frostbitten guitar noise. Instead of sorrowful gothic gloom, it evokes a flickering, fluorescent-lit purgatory where spectral children's games are played out with black-metal malice. The vocals are not typical roars, but wet, cavernous gasps that sound like they are echoing up through a dry-walled ventilation shaft.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 170–190 BPM, driven by erratic, blast-beat drum machine patterns ripped from a modified Galaga sound chip, clashing with the bouncy, deadpan mathematics of the xylophone.
- Harmonic Palette: Locrian and diminished modes played entirely in high registers, emulating the default demo-mode jingles of defunct arcade hardware warped by dying lithium batteries.
- Production Signatures: 1) Direct-injection arcade cabinet board feedback routed through tube guitar amplifiers, 2) Mic placement inside empty decorative indoor fountains to capture natural, dripping acoustic decay, 3) Pitch-shifted xylophone mallets striking metal chassis parts instead of wooden bars.
- Dynamics: Abrupt transitions from the muffled, distant hum of a food court security gate closing to a blinding, high-frequency wall of tremolo and mallets that feels physically claustrophobic.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the subterranean service corridors beneath a decaying Midwest shopping center left to rot after the 2008 retail collapse, a splinter faction of DIY sound-punks known as the "Escalator Cult" set up permanent camp. Wearing oversized mall-security windbreakers patched with corpsepaint corpse-paint and thrifted Sears uniforms, they broke open abandoned Pac-Man and Time Crisis cabinets, wiring the cathode-ray tube deflection coils into guitar pedals and mounting acoustic xylophones to the top of broken ice-cream kiosks. Their rituals involve holding speedrun competitions on dead arcade hardware while burning receipt tape as incense, dancing in jerky, robotic pantomimes beneath flickering LED exit signs.

Typical Instruments: Modified arcade cabinet sound chips, concert xylophone played with steel knitting needles, tremolo-picked jazzmaster guitars, cassette-tape dictaphones, and corroded cymbals.

For Fans Of: Leviathan, Xiu Xiu, Sunn O))), Mortiis

